<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asunción</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asunción is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>, on the east bank of the Paraguay river. Founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-salazar">Juan de Salazar</a> in 1537, it was for decades the centre of Spanish settlement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> region, and from it <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> was refounded in 1580. It became the capital of independent Paraguay in 1811.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asuncion.jpg" alt="Asunción" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MosheA · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Palacio_de_los_L%C3%B3pez_Asunci%C3%B3n_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Asunción lies on the eastern bank of the Paraguay River, just north-northeast of the confluence where the Pilcomayo joins it. The river and the Bay of Asunción separate the city from the Occidental Region of Paraguay and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>. The land around the bay was once covered with forests of the Humid Chaco, with trees often more than 40 m tall. The climate is humid subtropical, with very hot, humid summers and mild winters.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The conquistador Juan de Ayolas, who died around 1537, may have been the first to visit the site, on his way up the Paraguay River in search of a passage to the mines of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-peru">Upper Peru</a>. Juan de Salazar y Espinosa and Gonzalo de Mendoza were sent to look for him and failed to find him. Salazar stopped at a bay on the river to resupply his ships, found the people friendly, and decided to build a fort there in August 1537. He named it Nuestra Señora Santa María de la Asunción, after the Feast of the Assumption on 15 August.</p><p>Relations between the Spanish settlers and the Guaraní shaped the early town. They included the cuñadazgo, a Guaraní institution by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> gave women to newcomers to seal alliances, and later violent mass seizures of women known as rancheadas. Asunción became the demographic and economic centre of the colony, and in 1545 a priest called it the paradise of Muhammad, a phrase that became an epithet of the city. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestizo">mestizo</a> population that grew from these unions gave rise to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a>'s predominantly mixed society. Spanish expeditions set out from Asunción to found other cities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> for the second time, Villarrica, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corrientes">Corrientes</a>, Santa Fe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and Santa Cruz de la Sierra, which is why the city is called the Mother of Cities.</p><p>Under Gaspar Rodríguez de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francia">Francia</a>, in office from 1813 to 1840, roads were built and streets named. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-antonio-lopez">Carlos Antonio López</a>, president from 1844 to 1862, more than 400 schools, metallurgical factories and the first railway in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> were built. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a> led the country through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguayan-war">Paraguayan War</a> of 1864–1870. Asunción fell to Brazilian forces on 1 January 1869, and Brazilian troops occupied it until 1876.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Asunción is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and forms an autonomous capital district outside any <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. It is the seat of the executive, legislative and judicial powers. Greater Asunción includes cities of the Central Department such as San Lorenzo and Luque. Spanish and Guaraní are both spoken in the city, together with the mixed Jopará, and about 90 per cent of its people are Catholic.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Asunción” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Paraguay grew out of the Spanish province governed from <b>Asunción</b>, founded in 1537, whose largely Guaraní-speaking mestizo population lived far from the centres of empire; from 1776 it belonged to the Viceroyalty of the Río de la Plata.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-paraguay">Republic of Paraguay · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Jesuits were expelled in the 18th century, the people of the reductions gradually moved north and west towards <b>Asunción</b>, and the mission Guarani and the Guarani of everyday use met at length for the first time; most speakers left the regulated mission variety for the one that had…</blockquote><a href="/language/guarani">Guarani · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the history of <b>Asunción</b> the Franciscan Luis de Bolaños translated the catechism into Guarani, and the language became the medium of mission life.</blockquote><a href="/culture/guarani">Guaraní · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li><li><blockquote>The northern part, with its capital at <b>Asunción</b>, became the Governorate of Paraguay, also known as Guayrá, covering the lands along the Paraguay and upper Paraná rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-paraguay">Governorate of Paraguay</a></li><li><blockquote>Juan de Salazar founded <b>Asunción</b> in 1537, upriver on the Paraguay river, and it became the capital of the governorate.</blockquote><a href="/state/governorate-of-the-rio-de-la-plata">Governorate of the Río de la Plata</a></li></ul></section>
